# Untitled

## Polyoxometalate Reinforced Perovskite Phase for High-Performance Perovskite Photovoltaics
Ionic hybrid perovskites face challenges in maintaining their structural stability against non-equilibrium phase degradation, therefore, it is essential to develop effective ways to reinforce their corner-shared \(\left[\mathsf{P b l}_{6}\right]^{4-}\) octahedral units. To strengthen structural stability, redox-active functional polyoxometalates (POMs) are developed and incorporated into perovskite solar cells (PSCs) to form a robust polyoxometalates/perovskite interlayer for stabilizing the perovskite phase. This approach offers several advantages: 1) promotes the formation of an interfacial connecting layer to passivate interfacial defects in addition to stabilize the \(\left[\mathsf{P b l}_{6}\right]^{4-}\) units through exchanged ammonium cations in POMs with perovskites; 2) facilitates continuous structural repairing of \(\mathsf{P O}^{0}\)- and \(\mathsf{I}^{0}\)-rich defects in the \(\left[\mathsf{P b l}_{6}\right]^{4-}\) unit through redox electron shuttling of the electroactive metal ions in POMs; 3) provides guidance for selecting suitable redox mediators based on the kinetic studies of POM's effectiveness in reacting with targeted defects. The POM-reinforced device maintains \(97.2\%\) of its initial PCE after \(1500\,\mathrm{h}\) of shelf-life test at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), while also enhancing the long-term operational stability. Additionally, this approach can be generally applicable across scalable sizes and various bandgap perovskites in devices, showing the promise of using functional POMs to enhance perovskite photovoltaic performance.
## 1. Introduction
Metal halide perovskite has garnered significant research attention as a promising light-absorbing material for energy
conversion. They have been employed in various configurations, including single-junction, all-perovskite tandem, perovskite-organic tandem, and silicon-perovskite tandem solar cells, achieving high certified power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) exceeding \(26\%\), \(28\%\), \(25\%\), and \(34\%\), respectively.[1–5] This success can be attributed to the defect tolerance of hybrid perovskites (corner-shared \(\left[\mathsf{P b l}_{6}\right]^{4-}\) octahedral unit) and their unique band structures. The bandgaps of perovskites derive from their antibonding orbitals at both the valence band maximum (VBM) and conduction band minimum (CBM).[6] Consequently, breaking these bonds produces states away from the bandgap, resulting in either shallow defects or states within the valence band. Although high defect tolerance allows polycrystalline perovskite films to have \(10^{6}\) times higher defect densities than single-crystal silicon while achieving comparable device performance,[7] high defect densities (e.g., antisites, interstitials, vacancies)[8] reduce the energy required for ion migration through the bulk and
grain boundaries.[9] Over time, this will lead to defect propagation and irreversible degradation of the perovskite phase.[10,11] To address the instability of hybrid perovskite under non-equilibrium conditions, it is necessary to reduce defects and reinforce the
corner-shared \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) octahedral unit through proper ionic and covalent bonds.
Although the growth of perovskite structure has been systematically optimized,[12,13] high defect densities \((\approx\!10^{17}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) are still prevalent in polycrystalline perovskite films, with A-site cation comprising most of the vacancy defects. These defects arise from the interrupted periodic growth of the \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) unit on surface and grain interfaces, as well as thermal-induced cation evaporation during perovskite preparation. Incorporating ammonium iodide additives could effectively mitigate both A-site and X-site vacancy defects while simultaneously strengthen the electrostatic interaction between ammonium cations and the \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) octahedral unit.[14] Although high binding affinity molecules to the \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) unit have been developed to enhance the decomposition energy of perovskite phase,[15] hybrid perovskites still encounter quite significant phase instability under thermal and illumination conditions.
The structural damages of corner-shared \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) unit often occur through the decomposition of \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) into \(\mathrm{BX}_{2}\) and AX due to external forces. This decomposition leads to further degradation, breaking \(\mathrm{BX}_{2}\) into metallic \(\mathrm{B}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{X}_{2}\) gas.[16,17] The process is influenced by breaking the halide bonds (particularly iodide bonds) in the VBM of perovskite structure. The iodide, oxidized by photogenerated holes, would generate neutral iodine interstitials and iodide vacancies. These iodine interstitials act as recombination centers with iodide vacancies mitigate toward interfaces due to their conductivity. This happens in conjunction with \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) gas escaping from the perovskite and iodine ions diffusing into charge-transporting layers, where they react with metal electrodes. While the thermodynamically favored reaction between \(\mathrm{B}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{X}^{0}\) drives the elimination of both defects, the kinetic barriers at room temperature limit the reaction's progress.
Electroactive additives provide a promising way of continuously healing B-site and X-site defects by oxidating the metallic B and reducing \(\mathrm{X}^{0}\). These additives include organic components (e.g., tributyl phosphine, 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide)[18,19] and inorganic salts (e.g., ion pair \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)-\(\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\)-\(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), and ferrocene).[20–22] However, the rapid halide escape rate and slow rate of electron shuttling tend to restrict their effectiveness in repairing the targeted defects. Therefore, designing functional mediators with optimized redox properties and defect passivation abilities is crucial for reinforcing the perovskite structure.
Based on this principle, we have identified both organic and inorganic components in polyoxometalates (POM) as promising candidates for repairing structural defects in perovskites and enhancing their stability. It involves two main design criteria: 1) incorporating POMs with appropriate metal substitutions to regulate their redox potential, thereby facilitating efficient redox kinetics to repair \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) defects in \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) unit; 2) modifying the cationic portion of the POMs with ammonium groups for passivating the A-site defects, thus creating a robust POM/perovskite interlayer to stabilize the \(\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}\) unit. Therefore, POM-reinforced perovskite, spanning both normal and wide bandgaps, showed enhanced stability against the aging tests. A 1.55-eV PSC achieves a high PCE of \(25.21\%\). Furthermore, the integration of POM interlayers into a wide bandgap perovskite-organic tandem solar cell yielded a quite encouraging PCE of \(24.86\%\).
## 2. Result and Discussion
## 2.1. Design Principles of POM Mediators to Reinforce Perovskite
Polyoxometalates (POMs) represent a class of anionic clusters composed of high-valent early transition metal ions polymerized with terminal or bridging oxygen atoms.[23,24] These nano-sized POM clusters possess versatile structures and unique physicochemical properties, enabling them to be used in perovskite photovoltaics: 1) n-type POMs, with low LUMO and work function values, facilitate carrier tunneling from the photovoltaic layer to the hole-selective layer, potentially enhancing interfacial carrier kinetics; 2) the counter cations in POMs can be tailored to passivate the perovskite structure; 3) the reversible redox activity of POMs, involving high-valent early transition metal ions as electron mediator,[25] enables the passivation of B-site and X-site defects through an electron-shuttling process that oxidizes \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and reduces \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) species. POMs and their derivative structures come in various types (e.g., Keggin, Dawson, and Anderson). In this study, we focus on the structurally reliable Keggin-type POMs, with a general formula \(\mathrm{X}_{3}\mathrm{PM}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\), which allows for POMs' reliable properties through molecular design. In this structure, the M sites are typically occupied by \(\mathrm{W}^{6+}\) or \(\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}\) ions, and the X site by a positively charged cation (Figure 1a). Our discussion on the design principles of POM mediators is divided into two parts: 1) the regulation of metal ions in POM to tailor redox potentials and facilitate \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}/\mathrm{I}^{0}\) electron shuttling; and 2) the functionalization of the cationic component in POMs to effectively passivate vacancy defect in the perovskite film.
We initially investigated the redox activity of POM with the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PM}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\) structure. For effective electron transfer between \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\), the redox potential \((\mathrm{E}^{0})\) of POM mediator should lie between the \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}/\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) \((-0.365\mathrm{~V~}\) vs NHE) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}/\mathrm{I}^{-}\) \((0.536\mathrm{~V~}\) vs NHE) couples.[3] Previous studies have shown that the first redox peak of POMs can be controlled by varying the ratio of metal ions,[26] offering a way to fine-tune the redox potential. This allowed us to explore the efficiency of POM-mediated electron shuttling between \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\). We prepared \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{6}\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{40}\), and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\) materials (Figure S1, Supporting Information) and measured their redox potentials using cyclic voltammetry curves, which yielded values of -0.060, 0.124, and \(0.380\mathrm{~eV}\), respectively (Figure S2, Supporting Information). These results suggest that POMs substituted with W, Mo, and \(\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{Mo}\) alloy can effectively mediate the redox reaction between \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) (Figure 1b). The proposed electron shuttling mechanism is illustrated in Figure 1c: \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}+\mathrm{POM}(\mathrm{M}^{6+})\rightarrow\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}+\mathrm{POM}(\mathrm{M}^{5+})\); \(\mathrm{POM}(\mathrm{M}^{5+})+\mathrm{I}^{0}\rightarrow\mathrm{POM}(\mathrm{M}^{6+})+\mathrm{I}^{-}\). To assess the effectiveness of electron transfer, we dispersed POM, \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\), and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) powders in a mixed solvent of dimethylformamide and isopropanol (1:10 volume ratio) by balancing the solubility of the raw powders and the precipitation of the resulting \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\). The formation of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (Figure S3, Supporting Information) under these reaction conditions confirms that the electron shuttling reaction is thermodynamically favorable, driven by negative Gibbs free energy \((\Delta\mathrm{G})\). Further discussion on the reaction kinetics, particularly the impact of metal substitution in POMs, is provided in the subsequent section.
We expanded our molecular design by substituting the \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)cation in POMs with functional ammonium cations, as
previous studies indicate that such modifications can enhance perovskite structure through chemical and field-effect passivation, thereby suppressing interfacial charge recombination and improving perovskite stability.[14] To achieve this, we selected common monoammonium and diammonium ligands for chemical passivation (piperidinium, PPD; phenethylammonium, PEA; o-fluorophenylammonium, oF-PEA) and field-effect passivation (piperazinium, PP; octamethylenediammonium, ODA) to modify the cationic component of POMs. These functionalized POMs were synthesized (Figure S4, Supporting Information) starting with \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\) and applied to the buried surface of the perovskite layer, enhancing defect passivation and structural stability of the perovskite on the light-incident surface.[27]
Photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY) measurements were conducted to evaluate the effectiveness of functional POMs in structural passivation (Figure 1d). A self-assembled monolayer (SAM)-coated glass served as the reference substrate. The PLQY value of the perovskite film on the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\)-capped substrate slightly exceeded that of the film on the reference substrate (4.1% vs 3.9%), suggesting that the abundant metal-oxidate framework in POM interacts with \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) [28] reducing non-radiative recombination at the buried surface and improving film quality. Functional POMs with ammonium cation further improved the PLQY of the perovskite films on the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\)-capped substrate, indicating that the improved film quality likely results from the interaction between cationic component of POMs and the perovskite layer (Figure 1e). These findings suggest that structurally tailored POMs are effective in facilitating electron shuttling between \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) defects to repair decomposed \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit and in forming a robust perovskite/POM interlayer to stabilize the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) framework. POM-reinforced perovskites are expected to show increased resistance to external forces that cause non-equilibrium phase degradation. The related
mechanism behind this stability is discussed in the following section.
## 2.2. Enhanced Control of POM-Mediated Electron Shuttling for Stable Perovskites
Redox mediators have recently garnered significant attention for their role in mitigating deep-level \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) defects in \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit. This section comprehensively explores POM-mediated electron shuttling and offers guidance on selecting appropriate redox mediators to reinforce the perovskite structure. To isolate the effect of cation substitution in POM, the focus was on H-terminated POM. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed on POM/perovskite interfacial models with FAI- and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-rich (100) termination to identify the reaction sites (Figure 2a). The lower binding energy of POM at the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-rich termination compared to the FAI-rich termination suggests potential reaction sites between POM and the exposed \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-rich surface in defective perovskite structures, indicating POM's targeted binding to \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-rich surfaces that commonly initiate the formation \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) defects under external forces.
The effectiveness of POMs in stabilizing perovskite films was evaluated by depositing the films onto SAM or POM/SAM substrates. Aged films were analyzed using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) to measure the \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) concentration on the buried surface. \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) species was excluded from the analysis due to its volatility, which can lead to inaccurate measurements. While the original perovskite films showed high quality with minimal detected \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) defects (Figure S5, Supporting Information), varying levels of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) defects were observed in the aged films. All POM variants reduced the \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) formation in aged films compared to the perovskite film on the pure SAM substrate, as evidenced
by the residual \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) levels (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Among them, the W-substituted POM was the most effective in preventing the \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) defects within the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit during aging (Figure 2b). Although all POM mediators with redox potential between \(\mathrm{E}^{0}(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}/\mathrm{Pb}^{2+})\) and \(\mathrm{E}^{0}(\mathrm{I}^{0}/\mathrm{I}^{-})\) were thermodynamically favorable for treating these targeted defects, their effectiveness in mitigating defects varied. Therefore, selecting suitable redox mediators based on their redox potential and defect repair capacity is crucial.
To understand the differences in POM-mediated electron shuttling reaction in defective \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit, we first conducted thermodynamic calculations simulating the direct reaction of \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) species. POMs with W and Mo substitutions, representing low and high redox potentials, respectively, were selected. The calculated \(\Delta G\) for the half-reactions
(Figure 2c; Figure S7 and Table S1, Supporting Information) illustrated that the redox capacity of POM effectively lowers the formation energy for both \(\Delta G\) \((\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Pb}^{2+})\) and to \(\Delta G\) \((\mathrm{I}^{0}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{I}^{-})\) , thereby facilitating the electron shuttling. However, the lower reaction energy with \(\mathrm{POM}(\mathrm{Mo})\) was inconsistent with its limited capacity to mitigate targeted \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) defects (Figure 2b), suggesting that this defect elimination process is not governed more by kinetic factors than thermodynamic constraints.
We further investigated the reaction kinetics by monitoring the evolution of \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) concentration in suspension solutions with the addition of \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) , \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) and POM powders. All reactions reached completion after sufficient time, though at different rates (Figure 2d). The reaction followed first-order exponential decay kinetics, described by the equation \(\mathrm{~I~}=\mathrm{~I_{0}~e^{-\alpha t}~}\) , where \(\alpha\) represents the
reaction rate coefficient (units of \(1/\mathrm{time}\)) and \(\mathrm{I}_{0}\) denotes the initial concentration of \({ }^{10}\) species (Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information). The calculated \(\alpha\) values (Figure 2e) revealed that W-substituted POM has the highest reaction rate in facilitating this electron shuttling process. In contrast, POM mediators with Mo or Mo/W substitutions did not significantly enhance the reaction kinetics, resembling the control case. These kinetic results align the capacity of POMs to mitigate defect formation in aged perovskite film, indicating POM-mediated electron shuttling is kinetically controlled.
The half-reactions were examined separately to uncover differences in kinetics, with the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) occurring before the subsequent reduction of \({ }^{10}\) during electron shuttling. Although POM(Mo) has a higher reduction potential than POM(W) to react with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\), the addition of \(\mathrm{Pb}\) powders to both POM(W) and POM(Mo) solutions resulted in a rapid color change from colorless \((\mathrm{M}^{6+}\) ion) to blue \((\mathrm{M}^{5+}\) ion) within a few seconds (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The fast electron shuttling rate in the \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}/\mathrm{POM}(\mathrm{W})\) system is possibly due to its stronger binding interaction compared to POM(Mo) case (Table S2, Supporting Information). The similar reaction kinetics of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) oxidation suggest that the reduction of \({ }^{10}\), rather than the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\), is the rate-limiting step in the POM-mediated electron shuttling process. To further investigate, \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) powders were introduced into the POM solutions containing \(\mathrm{Pb}\) powders that had reacted for several minutes. The higher reaction rates observed with POM(W) compared to POM(Mo) showed that the \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) reduction is critical in completing the electron shuttling process (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Thus, a redox mediator with a greater driving force for \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) reduction is advantageous. Among the Keggin-type POM studied, W-substituted POM shows promise in effectively suppressing the formation of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \({ }^{10}\) defects in the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit in aged films (Figure 2f), owing to its favorable reaction kinetics.
The stability of perovskite films deposited on different substrates was evaluated through continuous laser illumination at room temperature and heating at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The control sample showed a rapid decline in PL intensity at \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\), illustrating the gradual degradation of \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) units within the perovskite phase. Conversely, the POM mediator effectively stabilized the perovskite structure through its well-designed electron-shutting mechanism. The mechanism also suppressed phase segregation in \(\mathrm{Br}/\mathrm{I}\) mixed perovskite structure.[3] Specifically, phase segregation in wide bandgap perovskite films \((1.78\ \mathrm{eV})\) could be effectively mitigated by the redox-active POM mediator (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Additionally, aged perovskite film grown on POM substrate showed fewer \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) defects compared to the control sample (Figure S14, Supporting Information). These findings indicate that functional POMs, particularly W-substituted POMs with effective electron-shutting capabilities, can mitigate deep-level defects of \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) units in non-equilibrium states, thereby stabilizing the perovskite phase. However, vacancy defects in A-site cations remain a concern, as illustrated in Figure 2f. The following discussion will address the role of cation designed in W-substituted POM structures.
## 2.3. Promoted Cation Exchange in POMs for Structurally Passivated Perovskites
The discussion centers on enhancing the interaction between POMs and perovskites to stabilize the perovskite phase through cation exchange reaction within the POM structure. Before delving into this, we first clarify the component distribution pattern of the SAM/POM composite. A POM containing \(\mathrm{W}^{6+}\) metal ions and \(\mathrm{PPD}^{2+}\) counter cations was selected due to W's rapid electron shuttling ability (as shown in Figure 2f) and PPD cation's defect passivation capability (as seen in Figure 1d). The POM solution was applied to the SAM-covered ITO substrate, and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping was conducted (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The observation of fragmented pinholes in the \(\mathrm{P}\) signal from the SAM film illustrated incomplete coverage of SAM onto the ITO substrate.[29–31] These voids could be effectively filled by nanosized POMs (Figure S16, Supporting Information) to form an intercalated SAM/POM pattern rather than a bilayer configuration, as suggested by the disordered distribution of W element in the SAM/POM sample.
Depth-dependent ToF-SIMS analysis (Figure 3a,b) revealed that the \(\mathrm{WO}_{3}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{PO}_{3}^{-}\)signals (representing POM and SAM, respectively) appeared almost simultaneously, confirming the intercalation of POMs into the SAM film to form an intercalated SAM/POM pattern. The uneven distribution of the \(\mathrm{WO}_{3}^{-}\)signal in the SAM/POM substrate suggests incomplete POM coverage on the SAM layer (Figure S17, Supporting Information), which does not hinder the contact between the perovskite and SAM layer. The intercalated POM/SAM pattern was further supported by alterations in contact angle (Figure S18, Supporting Information) and work function measurements (Figure S19, Supporting Information), with values positioned between those of the ITO/SAM and ITO/POM configurations.[32] This intercalated structure allows POM to effectively fill voids in the SAM film, thereby stabilizing it against solvent erosion during the perovskite layer's solution processing.
Notably, a significant difference in the perovskite signal \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2}^{-})\) was observed between the ITO/SAM/PVK and ITO/SAM/POM/PVK samples, as indicated by the dashed line in Figure 3a,b. Compared to the control, the slower decrease in the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}^{-}\)signal near POM in the ITO/SAM/POM/PVSK sample suggests an interaction between the perovskite and POM, leading to the formation of an interlayer at the buried interface (Figures S20 and S21, Supporting Information). Similarly, a delayed signal of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}^{-}\)was observed when perovskite was deposited on the SAM/POM substrate with POM containing mixed W and Mo metals (Figure S22, Supporting Information). However, increasing the concentration of POM solutions during deposition did not yield additional signals from perovskite-POM adducts (Figure S23, Supporting Information). To investigate this interaction, we have conducted experiments using POMs with different cations: 1) peeling off the buried perovskite surface from the SAM/POM(oF-PEA) substrate for XPS measurement revealed an F signal without any W signals, suggesting that the oF-PEA+ cation potentially reacted with perovskite and integrated into its lattice (Figure S24, Supporting Information); 2) stirring a dispersion of POM(oF-PEA) and FAI powders produced a new component distinct from the original reactants, with \({ }^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR
revealing that a significant amount of \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) cation replaced the oF-PEA+ cation in the POM(oF-PEA) structure (Figure S25, Supporting Information); 3) adding POM(PEA) to the perovskite precursor solution during film preparation led to the formation of low-dimensional \(\mathrm{PEA}_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{4}\) (Figure S26, Supporting Information). These findings confirm cation exchange between at perovskite/POM interface, leading to the formation of an interlayer that effectively passivates defects and heals the buried surface.
The interlayer formed between the substrates and perovskite films can potentially influence the quality of the films. To investigate this, perovskite films deposited on different substrates were peeled off and analyzed using grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS; Figure 3c) at two incident angles: \(0.1^{\circ}\) for surface analysis and \(0.30^{\circ}\) for bulk analysis. A significant distinction was observed in the Debye-Scherrer-like ring associated with the perovskite and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) phases (Figure 3d), and the diffraction
patterns in the out-of-plane direction were compared. At the \(0.1^{\circ}\) incident angle, the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) intensity showed minimal variation, consistent with previous analyses. However, the (001) signal intensity of perovskite on the SAM/POM substrate was notably stronger than in the control sample, indicating that the formed interlayer at the buried interface facilitated the orientated growth of perovskite crystals (Figure 3e). Likewise, the enhanced crystal orientation in the bulk phase facilitated by the SAM/POM substrate resulted in a uniform and compact grain morphology on the top surface of the perovskite film (Figure S27, Supporting Information).
Furthermore, the perovskite film deposited on the SAM/POM substrate demonstrated a low level of energetic disorder at the band edge (Figures S28, Supporting Information). Spectral characterization showed that perovskite on the SAM substrate exhibited higher PL intensity compared to ITO, likely attributed to defect passivated by SAM functional groups. However, the formation of POM/perovskite interlay on the SAM/POM composite augmented even higher perovskite fluorescence (Figure S28c, Supporting Information). Spatially distributed PL mapping showed that the perovskite film grown on the POM substrate had uniform PL intensity from bottom to top, outperforming the film grown on the SAM substrate (Figure S29, Supporting Information). The improved structural integrity and crystal quality of perovskite films were further supported by the longer carrier lifetime (Figure 3f) and reduced structural defects (Figure 3g).
Based on the analysis of POM's interaction with perovskite, two key insights emerge regarding POM-reinforced perovskite phases: 1) the functional groups of POM's cations can interact with perovskite to form a robust interlayer to effectively stabilize the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) framework at the buried surface; 2) the redox activity of the metal-oxide framework in POM facilitates electron shuttling, enabling both the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and the reduction of \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) defects generated during aging. This stabilizes the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) framework under non-equilibrium conditions through both thermodynamic and kinetic processes. Therefore, the application of POM offers a promising approach for achieving high-performance perovskite solar cells.
## 2.4. POM-Enhanced Photovoltaic Performance in PSCs
Inverted PSCs were fabricated using the device configuration of ITO/SAM/POM/Perovskite/ \(\mathrm{\DeltaC_{60}/}\) bathocuproine \((\mathrm{BCP})/\mathrm{Ag}\) to assess their photovoltaic performance, with a POM-free device as the reference. The concentration of POM was optimized (Figure S30a; Table S3, Supporting Information), and the device performance with different POM variants is presented in Figure 4a. Devices with SAM, SAM/POM(H), SAM/POM(PP), SAM/POM(PPD), SAM/POM(PEA), SAM/POM(oF-PEA), and SAM/POM(ODA) substrates achieved performance values of \(22.94~\pm~0.14\) , \(23.21~\pm~0.23\) , \(24.09~\pm~0.12\) , \(24.26~\pm~0.07\) ,
\(23.46 \pm 0.14\), \(24.21 \pm 0.10\), and \(24.04 \pm 0.10\), respectively, demonstrating that the functional groups in POMs can effectively enhance device performance. The stabilized power output of the devices closely matched the PCEs obtained from \(J{-}V\) measurements (Figure S30b, Supporting Information). The SAM/POM configuration also demonstrated broad applicability for enhancing device performance (Table S4, Supporting Information). Specially, 1) incorporating silicotungstic acid with \(\mathrm{PPD}^{2+}\) as a cation into the SAM charge-selective layer resulted in improved FF and PCE parameters compared to the control (Figure S31a,b); 2) a commercially available SAM (i.e., Me-4PACZ) combined with POM can outperform the pure SAM substrate (Figure S31c, Supporting Information). Further performance enhancement was achieved by adding an antireflective layer to the glass substrate and employing a bilayer passivation strategy.[33] The champion device reached a PCE of \(25.22\%\) in reverse scan, with a Voc of \(1.166\,\mathrm{V}\), Jsc of \(25.22\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), and FF of \(85.73\%\) (Figure 4b; Table S5, Supporting Information), marking the best performance for redox mediator-based PSCs (Table S6, Supporting Information). The integrated photocurrent derived from the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum closely aligned with the \(J{-}V\) measurements, and the perovskite bandgap was calculated as \(1.55\,\mathrm{eV}\) using EQE derivative analysis (Figure S32, Supporting Information).
Depositing perovskite film on hydrophobic SAM substrates typically presents challenges, often leading non-uniform films with numerous defects. However, the combined polarity and conductivity of POM with SAM not only significantly improves substrate coverage,[32] but also effectively passivates perovskite defects at the buried interface to enable the achievement of high-performance perovskite solar modules. A perovskite minimodule \((11.7\mathrm{-cm}^{2}\) aperture area) using SAM/POM configuration could achieve a high PCE of \(22.12\%\) (Figure 4c). This module was also certified through an independent photovoltaic certification laboratory to achieve a PCE of \(21.45\%\) (Figure S33, Supporting Information), representing state-of-the-art performance for inverted perovskite mini-modules.
Furthermore, POMs can be incorporated into the perovskite precursor solution during film preparation to passivate defects and enhance device stability under harsh aging conditions (Figure S34 and Table S7, Supporting Information). Devices incorporating POM both at the buried surface and within the bulk, using ALD \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) to replace the BCP as electron-transporting layer, were fabricated to test their stability. The unencapsulated POM-based device could maintain \(97.2\%\) of its initial PCE after \(1500\,\mathrm{h}\) of shelf-life testing at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), compared to \(90\%\) of the control device (Figure 4d). These devices were further encapsulated with UV-curable adhesive and subjected to more rigorous conditions (continuous MPP tracking under 1-sun-equivalent LED illumination at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). Although both the POM-based device and control device showed certain performance degradation during testing, likely due to electrode oxidation and erosion, ion migration (including upward migration of \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ion and the downward migration of metal ion), degradation of electron transport layer, and ineffective encapsulation, the POM-based device demonstrated much better stability.[34] Notably, the encapsulated POM-based device could retain nearly \(80\%\) of its initial PCE after \(495\,\mathrm{h}\), whereas the control device lost over \(40\%\) of its initial PCE after just \(230\,\mathrm{h}\). Cross-sectional SEM images of
aged devices revealed noticeable pinhole formation in the active layer of the control sample, while the POM-reinforced perovskite layer maintained better morphology (Figure S35, Supporting Information). These results underscore the advantage of functional POMs in enhancing film quality and stabilizing the perovskite structure.
## 2.5. General Applicability of Functional POMs in Different Bandgap Devices
Motivated by the positive effects of POMs in defect passivation and structural stabilization of the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit in the perovskite structure, we extended the application of POMs to wide-bandgap (WBG) perovskite films (1.68 and \(1.78\,\mathrm{eV}\)). Single-junction WBG PSCs were subsequently fabricated.[35] The POM-based devices exhibited higher PCEs than the control case, achieving PCEs of \(21.82\%\) and \(19.75\%\), respectively (Figures S36 and S37, Supporting Information). Previous studies have reported that the incorporation of POM can induce p-doping in polymer donors within organic solar cells (OSCs) to increase hole extraction.[32] Expanding on this knowledge, an OSC featuring a PM6:BTP-eC9 active layer, a SAM/POM \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\right.\) was utilized) charge-selective layer, and a PNDIT-F3N electron-transporting layer was fabricated to achieve a PCE of \(18.46\%\) (Figure S38, Supporting Information).
Given POM's effectiveness in enhancing both PSCs and OSCs, it was also applied in the fabrication of perovskite/organic tandem solar cells (PO-TSC). To optimize photon utilization and current matching between the two subcells, a \(1.78\,\mathrm{eV}\) perovskite active layer was utilized as the bottom cell, while a \(1.38\,\mathrm{eV}\) organic active layer served as the top cell (Figure S39a, Supporting Information). The PO-TSC achieved a remarkable PCE of \(24.86\%\) with matched current density between the perovskite and organic subcells (Figure S39b,c, Supporting Information). Moreover, the unencapsulated PO-TSC could maintain \(98\%\) of its initial PCE after \(11\,\mathrm{h}\) of continuous operation under one-sun illumination at \(\approx45\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-filled atmosphere (Figure S39d, Supporting Information).
## 3. Conclusion
We have developed a series of structurally and functionally tailored organic-inorganic hybrid polyoxometalates to be incorporated with SAMs, aimed at improving the efficiency and stability of perovskite photovoltaics. This inclusion of POM served multiple functions: 1) metal substitutions in POMs help regulate electron shuttling and redox kinetics, aiding in the repair of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{0}\) defects within the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit; 2) modified cations in POM facilitate interactions with the perovskite structure, enabling defect passivation and stabilization of the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) unit at the contacting interface. This approach is highly effective in improving film quality for both normal- and wide-bandgap perovskites, as well as organic active layers. Consequently, the integration of functional POMs in devices incorporating these perovskites resulted in enhanced efficiencies, stability, and scalability. Overall, this study highlights the potential of designing functional POMs to reinforce the perovskite structure, thereby increasing the performance and durability of perovskite photovoltaics.
